= check-notify-daemon-presence-only
f lib/mux-notify_lib
t mux-check
# A BUS THAT ANSWERS "NOBODY OWNS IT" IS A FINDING, not an unknown. Folding
# `false` into the cannot-tell answer restores the exact bug this probe was
# written for: notify-send present, no daemon owning the name, every
# notification dying with `Message recipient disconnected from message bus
# without replying`, and `mux check` reporting [OK] about it. A presence check
# that passes on a fully installed and fully broken box is the failure the
# marker contract exists to prevent, so having it inside the file that defines
# that contract was the worst available place for it.
# RE-POINTED when the `false` arm stopped returning directly: it now falls
# through to the activatable question, so the line that carries the finding is
# the one that keeps `false` OUT of the cannot-tell answer.
-       *false*) ;;
+       *false*) continue ;;
m a missing notifier was not reported

= check-notify-daemon-unasked-is-broken
f libexec/mux-check
t mux-check
# BEING UNABLE TO ASK IS NOT EVIDENCE OF A BROKEN DESKTOP. `mux check` over
# ssh reaches no session bus, so treating the third answer as "nothing will
# display it" is a confident false finding about a machine that is fine: the
# same mistake the WAYLAND_DISPLAY validator shipped the same week, which
# judged its own environment rather than the value it was handed. The test
# asserts the ABSENCE of the warning, because the bug is an extra one.
#
# TARGETED AT THE HEDGE BRANCH rather than at the probe call, because the
# corpus refused the first attempt: setting the verdict directly makes an
# EARLIER case (notify-send with no closer, where the answer is also
# cannot-tell) take the new branch, so the kill landed on the missing-closer
# assertion and proved nothing about this one.
-   elif [ "$_nd" -eq 2 ]; then
+   elif false; then
